# Git LFS, by FILE TYPE rather than by path.
#
# A path rule (`presentations/the-rectangle/audio/*.wav`) was the first
# version and it is the same defect as a hand-maintained list: the next talk
# in the next directory gets plain-git binaries and nobody notices until the
# clone is huge. The type is the durable fact — a wav is a wav wherever it
# lands.
#
# WHY THE AUDIO IS COMMITTED AT ALL. The Rectangle's 24 rendered takes look
# like derived output and mostly are, but every **DURATION:** marker in its
# NARRATION.md is measured with ffprobe against these exact files. A clone
# that re-renders instead of fetching gets a script whose timings describe
# audio it does not have. Piper is deterministic enough to sound the same and
# not enough to be the same file.
#
# WHY LFS. 46MB of binary, and re-cutting the script rewrites all 24 — that
# is history every clone would carry forever.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY:
#
#   *.gif, *.png — 21 GIFs (7.3MB) and 9 PNGs are ALREADY committed as plain
#     git objects. Adding them here would not convert those; it would only
#     convert the next one touched, leaving the same file type stored two
#     ways. Doing it properly means `git lfs migrate` and a history rewrite,
#     which is a repo-wide decision and not this branch's to make. If someone
#     takes it, the docs-and-demos workflow re-records every GIF, so it is the
#     natural moment.
#
#   voices/ — 224MB of piper's models are gitignored outright, not stored.
#     Third-party, re-downloaded by say.sh on first run, not ours to
#     redistribute.
#
#   the .txt receipts beside each wav — deliberately ordinary git objects.
#     Small, diffable, and present.py compares them to decide a take is
#     stale; in LFS a fresh clone could not run that check without fetching
#     the very thing it is checking.
#
# CI does not fetch these. actions/checkout defaults to lfs:false, so its
# working copy has pointer files — correct, because nothing in the build or
# the test suite reads presentations/. A contributor without git-lfs still
# gets a working clone: the wavs are pointer text, `say.sh all` re-renders
# them, and only the exact timings are lost.
*.wav filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
